// Every root appears once, in the section where it matters most for the exam; its example terms can come from anywhere in the course.
// Section: [name, rows, { "sub": why these roots matter, "exam": [[question kind id (the blueprint in data/index.js), how these roots help], ...] }]
// Row: [root, meaning, origin, [[term, what the term means when you put the parts together], ...], optional { "auto": false }]
// { "auto": false }: the root shows on the cards of its own example terms, but Eureka's dictionary never guesses it inside other words.
// Mastered/review marks are stored by root text (orgo-roots-v1), so keep a root's spelling when you edit it.
// An exam line names each root it relies on, and that is how the page lists the roots that help a question kind: a hyphenated
// root as written ("sub-", "-ide"), a bare-word root next to a bracket or a "+" ("trans (across)", "(axis", "carbo- + anion"),
// R / S and E / Z by their Latin and German words, and p as "p means".
// Read by the Word Roots page (roots/index.html), by Eureka's dictionary (shared/lookup.js) and by tools/build.js, which gives
// the class dictionary's entries their word parts from here. node tools/check-roots.js checks it.
